1
00:00:00,210 --> 00:00:01,335
Have you ever wondered?

2
00:00:01,350 --> 00:00:03,555
A whole hot rotisserie chicken

3
00:00:03,570 --> 00:00:05,200
Why is it so cheap?

4
00:00:05,430 --> 00:00:08,260
It is easier than cooking one yourself.

5
00:00:08,490 --> 00:00:10,635
Does the store profit from the chicken?

6
00:00:10,650 --> 00:00:11,380
Not necessarily.

7
00:00:11,730 --> 00:00:13,395
Because what the store wants to sell you

8
00:00:13,410 --> 00:00:15,280
may be much more than a chicken.

9
00:00:15,450 --> 00:00:17,440
Here is a business strategy.

10
00:00:17,550 --> 00:00:19,250
Loss Leader Strategy

11
00:00:20,070 --> 00:00:21,700
Loss Leader Strategy

12
00:00:21,810 --> 00:00:24,195
Low prices attract shoppers.

13
00:00:24,210 --> 00:00:26,200
Other products can earn a contribution.

14
00:00:26,370 --> 00:00:27,615
Let us do the math.

15
00:00:27,630 --> 00:00:29,320
All these numbers are hypothetical.

16
00:00:29,610 --> 00:00:31,420
A chicken sells for $6.

17
00:00:31,470 --> 00:00:33,100
Its direct cost is $7.

18
00:00:33,270 --> 00:00:34,335
For each chicken sold

19
00:00:34,350 --> 00:00:35,560
the store loses $1 at first.

20
00:00:35,670 --> 00:00:36,795
But once shoppers enter

21
00:00:36,810 --> 00:00:39,040
they also buy salad, drinks and bread.

22
00:00:39,510 --> 00:00:41,055
Suppose these extra items

23
00:00:41,070 --> 00:00:42,460
sell for $20 in total.

24
00:00:42,570 --> 00:00:45,520
Subtract $12 in variable costs.

25
00:00:45,630 --> 00:00:47,440
Their contribution is $8.

26
00:00:47,490 --> 00:00:49,840
Subtract the $1 chicken loss.

27
00:00:49,950 --> 00:00:51,075
This shopping basket

28
00:00:51,090 --> 00:00:52,720
contributes $7 in total.

29
00:00:53,010 --> 00:00:54,820
But here is the crucial question.

30
00:00:55,050 --> 00:00:57,195
Did the cheap chicken cause shoppers

31
00:00:57,210 --> 00:00:58,815
to buy these extra items?

32
00:00:58,830 --> 00:01:00,280
If they planned to buy them anyway

33
00:01:00,390 --> 00:01:01,695
we cannot attribute all that contribution

34
00:01:01,710 --> 00:01:03,315
to the chicken promotion.

35
00:01:03,330 --> 00:01:05,500
That is the key to business analysis.

36
00:01:05,850 --> 00:01:07,335
So a cheap chicken

37
00:01:07,350 --> 00:01:08,680
may be more than dinner.

38
00:01:08,790 --> 00:01:10,840
It may also attract shoppers.

39
00:01:11,010 --> 00:01:12,105
What really matters

40
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:13,960
is not the profit on one chicken.

41
00:01:14,130 --> 00:01:15,315
It is whether this strategy

42
00:01:15,330 --> 00:01:16,960
generates incremental profit.

43
00:01:17,010 --> 00:01:18,940
This is Business Through Data.
